#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# count_roots

#% text
# `count_roots(p, x)` gives the number of real roots of the polynomial `p` in `x`, counted with multiplicity.

#% text
# `count_roots(p, [x, a, b])` counts the roots in the closed interval from `a` to `b`, or, when `a` or `b` is complex, in the closed rectangle of the complex plane with corners `a` and `b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The coefficients may be algebraic numbers (radicals, root objects); the ends of an interval may be rational or real algebraic numbers, `-inf` or `inf`.

#% item
# Real roots are isolated exactly: with algebraic coefficients by Descartes' rule in ball arithmetic, each root then identified as a root of an irreducible factor of the norm, with exact arithmetic over the number field when the balls cannot decide. Comparisons with the ends are exact.

#% item
# Rectangles take rational (or Gaussian rational) corners and polynomials with rational coefficients. Roots on the boundary count, and are found exactly on each side; the other roots are placed by certified enclosures, so no count depends on a tolerance.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
count_roots(x^5 - 4*x^3 + x - 1, x)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
count_roots(x^5 - 4*x^3 + x - 1, [x, 0, 3])
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Multiplicities count, and so do roots at the ends:

#% cell
count_roots((x - 1)^3*(x + 2), [x, -2, 1])
#% output
#= 4

#% text
# Algebraic coefficients and ends:

#% cell
count_roots(x^4 - sqrt(5)*x^2 + 1/2, [x, -sqrt(2), sqrt(2)])
#% output
#= 4

#% text
# A rectangle in the complex plane:

#% cell
count_roots(x^8 - 1, [x, -1/2, 2 + 2*im])
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
count_roots(x^4 + 4, [x, -inf, inf])
#% output
#= 0

